WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.192 --> 00:00:02.671
(upbeat music)

00:00:02.671 --> 00:00:03.600
- As you know,

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.610
there have been lots of layoffs

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.530
even from the biggest tech companies.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.960
Meta laid off more than 11,000 employees,

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.690
Amazon cut about 18,000 jobs,

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.010
and Google just cut 6%

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.960
which is 12,000 employees.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.810
And that includes the
31 massage therapists

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.103
that got let go.

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:22.140
The Google employees who managed

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.460
to avoid mass layoffs

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.132
may have to continue
doing their jobs without

00:00:25.132 --> 00:00:26.973
on-site massages.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.760
My condolences.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.921
Not only is it shocking

00:00:30.921 --> 00:00:33.930
to see Google lay off 12,000 employees,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.020
but the way they did it was also shocking.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.810
Let's watch this clip
from a Google employee.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:42.030
- [Nicole] A day in my life
getting laid off at Google.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.460
So I woke up to this really
ominous text from my boss

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.680
and I honestly had no idea
what it was gonna be about,

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.270
so I called her the minute I woke up

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.103
and saw this,

00:00:49.103 --> 00:00:51.210
and she told me to check
the news and my email.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.332
So I rushed downstairs to find out

00:00:52.332 --> 00:00:55.230
that I had lost access
to basically everything.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.200
I couldn't log into my email
or even check my calendar.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.280
I called my boss back

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.810
and we just sobbed over the phone

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.880
because she was also
finding out about my layoff

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.110
for the first time today too.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.330
I started getting calls from
a bunch of my coworkers,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.090
and started finding out who
else was let go on my team

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.710
and some neighboring teams as well.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.041
But I think the worst part is

00:01:12.041 --> 00:01:15.240
that it seems like no one was
consulted on this decision

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.470
and everyone was just finding out

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.300
about the layoffs at the same time.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.820
It just felt like a really
bad game of Russian Roulette

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.560
and there was no consistency around

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.610
who was let go.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.590
It was also not performance based,

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.160
so it just felt really random.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.000
- It seemed like there
was no heads-up at all,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.460
no conversation with your manager,

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.690
and the layoffs felt pretty random

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.760
as it wasn't even based on performance.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.810
And then one day,

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.880
your access to everything gets cut off

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.311
immediately without communication,

00:01:41.311 --> 00:01:43.443
just like the way my ex broke up with me.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.990
I think a lot of these companies

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.940
simply overhired and overspent

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:48.930
during the pandemic,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.103
and are now finally addressing it.

00:01:51.103 --> 00:01:54.150
Generally, you want to avoid doing layoffs

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.940
because it looks so bad for your company.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.463
But since everyone else is doing it now,

00:01:58.463 --> 00:02:00.330
it became, kind of like,

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.340
socially acceptable to do so.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.810
So every company jumps

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.310
at the chance to fire people

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.100
without the blame for
handling things poorly.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.428
Here's an analogy.

00:02:09.428 --> 00:02:11.048
If you're in a classy restaurant

00:02:11.048 --> 00:02:14.430
and you're holding the
biggest fart of your life,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.470
you're going to want to keep that fart in

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.910
even if it kills you,

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.209
because you don't want to be known

00:02:19.209 --> 00:02:22.260
as the guy who farts at fancy restaurants.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.600
But if suddenly, everyone around you

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.940
is ripping out massive farts,

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:28.410
then you don't feel so bad letting one

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.000
out yourself anymore.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.390
Basically, if you fart
alone, you're a villain.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.150
But if you fart in
solidarity with the people,

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.070
now you're suddenly a victim

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.470
and farting is an act of protest.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.140
And together, you can turn
the blame on the food,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.713
or the restaurant or on
macroeconomic conditions.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.350
Was there anything that these tech giants

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:50.340
could have done differently

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.110
to prevent these massive layoffs?

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.149
Was there another solution?

00:02:54.149 --> 00:02:56.130
To even start answering these questions,

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.140
I think the first thing we have to ask

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.960
is why these layoffs even happen.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.630
I think it all comes down to leverage.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.690
And tech companies were over-leveraged.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.070
Leverage is a tool,

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.440
it helps you get more
out of what you have.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.060
Leverage comes in many forms.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.320
The simplest form of leverage

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.440
would be to take out a
loan to invest in stocks.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.100
Tech startups also take
on a form of leverage.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.450
Startups will raise money,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.850
spend money to hire more people,

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.320
subsidize their services,

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.700
operate at a loss.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.500
They're willing to lose money

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.960
in the beginning for the
sake of growing quickly

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.190
and capturing the market,

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.340
even before they figure
out their unit economics.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.317
If the startup gets to a
stable state and is profitable,

00:03:38.317 --> 00:03:39.900
then it's all good.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:40.860
So in this case,

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.140
taking on leverage was totally worth it.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.400
Investors are happy since

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.387
they made their money back faster.

00:03:46.387 --> 00:03:48.900
But what happens when you never get

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.270
to profitability and your business model

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.610
simply doesn't work?

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.830
Then you get a startup like Bird,

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.300
the electric scooter sharing company

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.460
that spawned dozens of copycats.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:00.810
At the peak in 2019,

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.660
the streets were littered with scooters.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.310
For a couple dollars, you could ride them

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:06.480
for 15 minutes.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.635
It was an awesome way
to get around the city,

00:04:08.635 --> 00:04:11.880
if you don't mind looking
like a suburban mall cop.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.500
But was it sustainable?

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.930
Was Bird profitable per ride?

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:16.763
No!

00:04:16.763 --> 00:04:18.570
In fact, it was also extremely dangerous,

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.640
which caused issues for the company.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:21.813
Let's look at a clip.

00:04:23.625 --> 00:04:26.958
(light classical music)

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:35.903
So you can see

00:04:35.903 --> 00:04:38.664
why electric scooter
companies get a bad rep.

00:04:38.664 --> 00:04:39.900
This guy clearly didn't

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.540
park his scooter back
in its designated area.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.330
He just left it there in
the middle of the street!

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:46.830
Without the cooperation of the customers,

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.273
it simply won't work.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.240
Also I've never seen anyone walk away

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.300
so casually after getting
hit by a car before.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.730
Anyway, buying scooters
and renting them out

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:58.590
was not a sustainable business.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.540
But VC money was flowing in

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.450
and scooter companies
expanded across the country,

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.112
and even internationally.

00:05:05.112 --> 00:05:09.450
At the peak, Bird was worth $2.3 billion

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.490
when it went public through a SPAC.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:14.280
And now, it's only worth $70 million,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.040
which means if you invested
$100 when it IPOed,

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.890
your shares would be now worth $3 today.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.980
How did a non-profitable
company get so overvalued?

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.290
Why did people even buy into that?

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:26.820
It's because in 2020,

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.500
the market was filled with greed.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.400
And SPACs were a vessel to
carry through that greed.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:33.660
SPACs were hot shit,

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.850
everyone wanted to invest in them.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.730
Normally, when a company
goes public through an IPO,

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.400
it must go through a
highly regulated process

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.630
that often takes over a year.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:46.020
Armies of investment bankers and lawyers

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.880
comb through the details of your company

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.090
to make sure they meet
the SEC's requirements.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.360
It's an expensive process
in terms of money, time

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.116
and just pure hassle internally.

00:05:56.116 --> 00:05:58.006
All that just so that you can penetrate

00:05:58.006 --> 00:06:01.860
through that SEC tunnel and IPO.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.830
But a SPAC can facilitate that process.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.320
A SPAC is a company with the bare minimum.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.810
A board, a management
team, a bank account.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:12.313
That company zips through the IPO process,

00:06:12.313 --> 00:06:14.700
and since there's literally nothing,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:15.870
it's a shell company.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.460
It's fast and cheap.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.590
Afterward, it raises money from people

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.210
looking to get rich quick,

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:22.860
to acquire a private company,

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.230
like Bird, so they can go public.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.090
SPACs allowed companies to go public

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.940
without the scrutiny of a real IPO.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.262
It was the perfect instrument
for Wall Street insiders

00:06:32.262 --> 00:06:35.389
to get rich by selling
off their shares to us

00:06:35.389 --> 00:06:38.730
at an inflated price because of the hype.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:40.650
And then the rest was history.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.907
2020 was the year of the SPAC.

00:06:42.907 --> 00:06:45.677
$250 billion were poured into them.

00:06:45.677 --> 00:06:47.780
The story might sound familiar because

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:50.730
the same thing happened with crypto.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.920
In retrospect, everybody was scrambling

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.600
for get-rich-quick schemes.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.700
Venture capital funds had so much money

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:57.540
to throw around,

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.130
the moment your startup pitch had the word

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.680
Web3, Creator Economy or Blockchain,

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:02.550
they jizz in their pants

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.230
and beg you to take their money.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.780
It was the "zero interest rate" phenomenon

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.478
meaning interest rates were so low

00:07:08.478 --> 00:07:11.670
that borrowing money was basically free,

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:14.250
which flooded the market
with all this extra cash,

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.747
and everything went up.

00:07:15.747 --> 00:07:19.350
But the music was going
to stop at some point.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.060
At that time, Ray Dalio,

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:23.167
a billionaire hedge fund manager said,

00:07:23.167 --> 00:07:25.080
"Cash is trash,"

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.550
because, during a bull market,

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:28.876
almost anything you invest in will go up

00:07:28.876 --> 00:07:31.290
because everyone else
is also investing in it.

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.510
So holding cash is
literally the worst thing

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:35.545
you can do for your financial health.

00:07:35.545 --> 00:07:37.200
Well, actually the worst thing

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.585
would be to spend it on a banana

00:07:38.585 --> 00:07:41.567
duct-taped to a wall for $120,000.

00:07:41.567 --> 00:07:44.370
Like it's gonna turn into compost,

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:45.570
what are you thinking?

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.977
Anyway, the overall sentiment then was,

00:07:47.977 --> 00:07:51.062
"If you're holding cash,
you're a fucking loser."

00:07:51.062 --> 00:07:52.740
I don't want to be a loser,

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:53.940
I don't want to fall behind.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:55.200
I see my friends getting rich

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.180
on meme stocks, crypto, NFTs.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:58.410
It doesn't matter.

00:07:58.410 --> 00:07:59.601
As long as it'll make me money,

00:07:59.601 --> 00:08:02.383
I'll drain my savings
account if I need to.

00:08:02.383 --> 00:08:05.520
Even the most conservative investors.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.020
Once we're in a bull market,

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.550
you can convince them to invest

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:10.633
in a cartoon picture of a rock.

00:08:10.633 --> 00:08:14.880
That JPEG sold for $1.3 million.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:17.700
That's more than this
photograph of a potato

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.160
which only sold for $1 million.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.889
At least this is was
a real tangible photo.

00:08:22.889 --> 00:08:26.370
And this potato looks pretty fucking sick.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.340
Basically, people rather be poor together

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:32.775
than feel left behind by
everyone else getting rich.

00:08:32.775 --> 00:08:36.270
That's the zero-interest rate epidemic.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:40.410
Now, big tech companies are
not immune to this epidemic.

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:41.970
They develop FOMO too,

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:43.650
the fear of missing out.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:45.060
Every tech company and their moms

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.500
are spending money to expand

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:47.760
and scale their businesses,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.620
you have no choice but to keep up

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.510
and also spend that cash.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:52.980
You don't want to be left behind.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.050
Remember, cash is trash.

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:56.460
So what did Google do?

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:58.560
They spent and hired aggressively,

00:08:58.560 --> 00:08:59.956
even if they didn't have a solid plan

00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:02.580
on how to deploy that capital effectively

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:05.610
or how to make use of
the talent they hired.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:07.620
That's why you see overqualification,

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:09.540
low-impact work, bloated teams,

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:12.900
and a bunch of employees
making day in the life TikToks.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.640
- [Narrator] Day in the
life as a 23-year old

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:16.680
product manager at Meta.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:18.540
I always journal in the morning

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.435
and then do a quick workout routine.

00:09:20.435 --> 00:09:21.660
I get dressed,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:22.803
I try to look cute every day.

00:09:22.803 --> 00:09:25.080
Got some food at the office.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.750
I make a coffee every single morning.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:29.518
I need that.

00:09:29.518 --> 00:09:30.900
I did some work on the roof.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:31.950
Worked until lunch

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:33.150
and then ate up there.

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:34.650
Here's me being cute.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:36.900
I got a snack, always.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:38.190
I then shuttled home,

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:39.570
the view is so pretty.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:41.010
Met up with some friends for dinner

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:42.450
and then my boyfriend came over,

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:44.823
and was acting like a DJ for my dog.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:47.850
That used to be me, man.

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.160
Now I have to actually
make YouTube videos.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:52.320
Now, things are starting to unwind.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:55.050
Ray Dalio no longer thinks cash is trash.

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:57.390
Interest rates are actually coming back

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:58.770
to a reasonable level.

00:09:58.770 --> 00:09:59.850
So these tech companies

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:01.650
have to undo what they did,

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:03.300
since their revenue couldn't keep up

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:04.947
with the pace of their spending.

00:10:04.947 --> 00:10:08.336
Though it was their
mismanagement of their leverage,

00:10:08.336 --> 00:10:12.063
the employees are the ones
who had to pay the price.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:14.656
All right, that's it for today.

00:10:14.656 --> 00:10:17.070
If you like these videos, let me know.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.710
If you don't, say that
you like them anyway

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:22.320
because it makes me
feel good about myself.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.303
Okay, I'll see you in the next video.

